// Regression test: `test::assert()` with zero arguments must be a clean compile
// error, not a compiler crash.
//
// History: `assert` used to be a compiler intrinsic registered with
// BuiltinInfo::Arity == -1 ("variadic/unknown") specifically because it
// genuinely accepts either 1 or 2 args (condition, optional message) -- not
// a single fixed count TypeChecker::visitFuncCall's generic single-arity
// check could express. That disabled arity checking for it entirely, and a
// 0-arg call reached CodegenIntrinsic's unbounds-checked Args[0] read and
// crashed the compiler (SIGABRT) -- see changelog/ for the original fix.
// `assert` is now an ordinary, real overloaded Turf function in
// lib/std/test.tr (two overloads: 1-arg and 2-arg) -- a 0-arg call is
// rejected by completely ordinary overload resolution ("no overload
// accepts that many [arguments]"), the same mechanism any other
// wrong-arity call to any function hits. Kept as a regression test to
// confirm that migration didn't quietly reopen the 0-arg case.

import test;

fn main(): int {
    test::assert();
    return 0;
}
